# NVCHAD TUTORIAL

This lesson teaches Neovim the way *your* config works: the NvChad setup in these dotfiles.
It is interactive: each exercise below is a pair of lines. The **first** line is yours to edit
until it matches the **second** one; a ✓ appears next to it when it does, a ✗ while it doesn't.
Do the exercises in order, and open the other tutorials any time with `:Tutor` (the classic
vimtutor) or `:Tutor tutor` (how these lessons are made).

Three things that are different in this config, and worth knowing before anything else:

  * The **leader key is the space bar**. `<leader>ff` means: space, then f, then f.
  * `;` opens the command line (it replaces `:`, so you type `;w` instead of `:w`).
    The old `;` (repeat the last f/t search) is gone; `,` still repeats it in reverse.
  * `jk`, typed quickly in insert mode, is the same as Escape.

Table of contents:

  1. Insert and escape
  2. Delete, change, repeat
  3. Counts and text objects
  4. Search and replace
  5. A tour of the NvChad keys
  6. When you are stuck

## LESSON 1: INSERT AND ESCAPE

Neovim has modes. You start in **Normal** mode, where keys are commands. `i` enters **Insert**
mode (typing inserts text); `Esc`, or `jk` here, goes back to Normal mode. `A` is "insert at
the end of the line", `I` at the start, `o` opens a new line below.

### Exercise 1.1  `A` appends at the end of the line

Press `A`, type the missing word, then `jk`.

say 
say hello

### Exercise 1.2  `I` inserts at the start of the line

Press `I`, type the missing word, then `jk`.

world
hello world


## LESSON 2: DELETE, CHANGE, REPEAT

Commands are built from an **operator** and a **motion**: `d` (delete) or `c` (change) plus
`w` (to the next word), `$` (to the end of the line), `iw` (this word)... Doubling an operator
acts on the whole line: `dd` deletes it.

### Exercise 2.1  `dw` deletes a word

Put the cursor on the extra word, then `dw`.

The quick brown brown fox jumps
The quick brown fox jumps

### Exercise 2.2  `x` deletes one character, `r` replaces one

Move onto the wrong letter and use `r`, then the right letter.

I lika coffee
I like coffee

### Exercise 2.3  `cw` changes a word

Put the cursor on `tea`, then `cw`, type `coffee`, then `jk`.

I like tea in the morning
I like coffee in the morning

### Exercise 2.4  `D` deletes to the end of the line

Put the cursor on the first space after `this`, then `D`.

keep this and remove the rest of it
keep this

### Exercise 2.5  `.` repeats the last change

Change the first `-` into `_` with `r_`, then jump to the next one and press `.`.

a-b-c-d
a_b_c_d


## LESSON 3: COUNTS AND TEXT OBJECTS

A number before a command repeats it: `d3w` deletes three words. A **text object** names a
piece of text: `iw` is a word, `i"` is the inside of a pair of quotes, `ip` a paragraph.
`ci"` means "change inside the quotes", which is one of the most useful commands there is.

### Exercise 3.1  A count: `d3w`

From the start of the line, delete three words with `d3w`.

very very very big dog
big dog

### Exercise 3.2  `ci"` changes what is inside quotes

Put the cursor anywhere inside the line, then `ci"`, type `right`, then `jk`.

answer = "wrong"
answer = "right"

### Exercise 3.3  `ci(` works for brackets too

Same idea: `ci(`, type `a, b`, then `jk`.

call(nothing)
call(a, b)

### Exercise 3.4  `gUU` makes the whole line uppercase

`gUU` upper-cases the line (`guu` lower-cases it).

make me loud
MAKE ME LOUD

### Exercise 3.5  `ye` copies a word, `p` pastes it

Copy `go` with `ye`, press `A`, type a space, `jk`, then paste with `p`.

go
go go


## LESSON 4: SEARCH AND REPLACE

`/word` searches forward, `n` goes to the next match, `N` to the previous one, `Esc` clears the
highlight. To replace, use the command line (remember: `;` opens it here):
`;s/old/new/g` replaces every `old` on the line, `;%s/old/new/g` in the whole file.

### Exercise 4.1  Replace on a line with `;s/cat/dog/g`

Type `;s/cat/dog/g` and press Enter.

the cat sat on the cat
the dog sat on the dog

### Exercise 4.2  Without the `g`, only the first match changes

Type `;s/red/blue/` and press Enter.

red car, red door
blue car, red door


## LESSON 5: A TOUR OF THE NVCHAD KEYS

These are not checked; try them in a real file, they are what you will use every day.
(`<leader>` is the space bar. Press space and wait: which-key shows what comes next.)

Files and search (Telescope):
    `<leader>ff`    find a file by name            `<leader>fw`    search text in all files
    `<leader>fb`    switch between open buffers    `<leader>fo`    recent files
    `<leader>fz`    search in the current file     `<leader>fh`    search the help pages
The file tree:
    `<C-n>`         toggle the tree                `<leader>e`     focus the tree
Buffers (open files) and windows:
    `<Tab>` / `<S-Tab>`   next / previous buffer   `<leader>x`     close the buffer
    `<C-s>`         save                           `<leader>b`     new buffer
    `<C-h/j/k/l>`   move between windows (and, in Herdr, between panes)
Editing:
    `<leader>/`     toggle comment (line or selection)    `<leader>fm`    format the file
    `<leader>n`     toggle line numbers                   `<leader>rn`    toggle relative numbers
Terminals:
    `<A-i>`         floating terminal              `<leader>h`     terminal below
    `<A-v>`         terminal on the right          `<C-x>`         leave terminal mode
Look:
    `<leader>th`    pick a color theme

## LESSON 6: WHEN YOU ARE STUCK

    `<leader>ch`    the NvChad cheat sheet: every default key, on one page
    `<leader>wK`    which-key: every key that is mapped right now, searchable
    `<leader>fh`    search the built-in help (`;help` followed by a word works too)
    `:Tutor`        the classic vimtutor, a longer walk through the same ideas

The best way to learn is to use it for real work and look things up when you are stuck.
When a command surprises you, press `u` to undo. You will want `u` a lot, and that is fine.
